-- `l4 batch` and the decision service write a default into a module of their own
-- as text, and read its names again there, at the top level of the file. A name
-- that is defined in a section AND at the top level means the section's where the
-- default is written and the top level's there, so the same input would give two
-- answers, with no error. It is refused for an exported rule
-- (TYPICALLY-ONE-BEHAVIOUR-SPEC.md W7, §4.3, silent review S6 of the second
-- review and rulings review S1). Written with its section, the name says which it is.

GIVETH A NUMBER
phi MEANS 1000

§ `Rates`
    -- ERROR: a section input's default naming the section's `phi`, which an
    -- exported rule below reads
    GIVEN scale IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY (phi PLUS 1)

GIVETH A NUMBER
phi MEANS 8

-- ERROR: an exported rule's input default naming the section's `phi`
@export default Pay
GIVEN base IS A NUMBER
      rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY (phi PLUS 1)
GIVETH A NUMBER
pay MEANS (base TIMES rate) PLUS scale

-- Fine: the same, with the section named. This rule does not read `scale`.
@export Pay, with the section named
GIVEN base IS A NUMBER
      rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY (`Rates`.phi PLUS 1)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`pay in the section` MEANS base TIMES rate
